\section{What the columns mean}{

The SPF publishes, for each survey quarter, a row of forecasts named
\code{TBILL1} to \code{TBILL6}. \code{TBILL1} is not a forecast at all: it is the previous
quarter's realised rate as known to respondents, and this function uses it
as an integrity check -- against the bundled \code{tbill_3m} it agrees to under
three basis points, which is what pins the column indexing down. \code{TBILL2} is
the current quarter and \code{TBILL3} to \code{TBILL6} are one to four quarters ahead.

Each forecast is a \emph{quarterly average} of a \emph{three-month} rate, so the
matching control settings are \code{survey_control(tenor = 3, average_months = 3)}.

The survey is collected in the middle of the quarter. \code{date} is set to the
middle month of the survey quarter, which is accurate to a couple of weeks
and is matched to the nearest panel observation anyway.
}

